      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. \"evolving requirements\" sounds way more professional than \"creep.\" i'll use that. i just want to make sure i'm prepared if he asks for specific examples of how things are changing. how do i describe those little extra tasks that just keep popping up and eating our time without making it sound like we lost control of the project?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When detailing those extra tasks, focus on the *impact* of the change, not the *act* of the change itself. Instead of saying, \"Someone asked for X, and now we have to do Y,\" try to group them thematically. For instance, you could say, \"We've seen a notable increase in the need for deeper integration testing across modules A and B, which wasn't initially scoped.\"\n\nThis makes it sound like a technical necessity or a necessary refinement, rather than a series of random requests. You are presenting data points about the project's increasing complexity, which is a very professional stance. It's like saying, \"The map we started with didn't account for this unexpected mountain range,\" instead of \"We got lost and wandered off the path.\"",
      "source": "target",
